{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & -2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2})=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} & -2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2})=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186430445 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186430446 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186430447 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(-1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2xy+{y}^{2} & {x}^{2}+2xy\\end{aligned}]$" "Find ${f}^{″}(-1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2xy+{y}^{2} & {x}^{2}+2xy\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186430447 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2xy+{y}^{2} & {x}^{2}+2xy\\end{aligned}],$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+2xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy)=2x+2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x+2y \\\\ 2x+2y & 2x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1,2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2xy+{y}^{2} & {x}^{2}+2xy\\end{bmatrix},$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+2xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy)=2x+2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x+2y \\\\ 2x+2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186430448 :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} & 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$" "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} & 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186430448 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} & 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=\\frac{2y}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2y}{x} & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=(-1)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} & 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=\\frac{2y}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2y}{x} & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=(-1)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186430711 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-21 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430712 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430713 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430714 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430719 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430721 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430723 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430724 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430726 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-23 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-23 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430728 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186430731 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186430731 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ -10{x}_{2}=40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-4.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-4)=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+(-4)=-1.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 40\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ -10{x}_{2}=40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-4.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(-4)=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+(-4)=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186430732 :question/problem "Find the vector $x$, if $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the vector $x$, if $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186430732 :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -24 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -24 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186430805 :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- **II.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- **III.** $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- **II.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- **III.** $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product"] [:db/cas 17592186430806 :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186430808 :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186430828 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186430828 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186430829 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186430829 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$