={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186431680 :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186431681 :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186431682 :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186431683 :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186431687 :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186431688 :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}