 of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186431705 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+7+0=9.\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+7+0=9.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186431707 :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by 