b/cas 17592186434950 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186434950 :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186434951 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ and $C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" "Let $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ and $C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$"] [:db/cas 17592186434951 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186434981 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-4-2x & x-4-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-4-2x=0 \\\\ x-4-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny-4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x+4\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4-2(2x+4) & =0 \\\\ -3x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=2x+4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-4\\,⟹\\,y=2(-4)+4=-4.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-4,-4)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-4-2y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4-2)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-4-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-4,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4,-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-4,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-4,-4)=det({f}^{″}(-4,-4))=(-2)^{2}-{1}^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-4,-4)=({f}^{″}(-4,-4))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-4,-4).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y-4-2x & x-4-2y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-4-2x=0 \\\\ x-4-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny-4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x+4\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4-2(2x+4) & =0 \\\\ -3x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=2x+4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-4\\,⟹\\,y=2(-4)+4=-4.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-4,-4)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-4-2y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4-2)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-4-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-4,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4,-4)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-4,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-4,-4)=det({f}^{″}(-4,-4))=(-2)^{2}-{1}^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-4,-4)=({f}^{″}(-4,-4))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-4,-4).$"] [:db/cas 17592186434982 :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186434982 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(xy-3)\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}xy-3=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nxy-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9{(\\frac{3}{x})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}-81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{3}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{-3}=-1,\\,\\,x=3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-3,-1)$ and $(3,1)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-6)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-6)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=-18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -18y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(3,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(3,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,1)=det({f}^{″}(3,1))=2\\cdot (-18)-{6}^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(3,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-3,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-3,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-3,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-3,-1)=det({f}^{″}(-3,-1))=(-2)\\cdot 18-(-6)^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(-3,-1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(xy-3)\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}xy-3=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nxy-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9{(\\frac{3}{x})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}-81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{3}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{-3}=-1,\\,\\,x=3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-3,-1)$ and $(3,1)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-6)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-6)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=-18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -18y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(3,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(3,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,1)=det({f}^{″}(3,1))=2\\cdot (-18)-{6}^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(3,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-3,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-3,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-3,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-3,-1)=det({f}^{″}(-3,-1))=(-2)\\cdot 18-(-6)^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(-3,-1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186434983 :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **III.** The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}12{x}^{2}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$\n\n- **III.** The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$"] [:db/cas 17592186434983 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,0)=det({f}^{″}(-1,0))=8\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)=({f}^{″}(-1,0))_{11}=8>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(-1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0,$ the point $(-1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,0)=det({f}^{″}(-1,0))=8\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)=({f}^{″}(-1,0))_{11}=8>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(-1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0,$ the point $(-1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186434984 :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣y>0\\},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x+\\frac{2}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x+\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{2}{-2x} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(-1)=2 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2).$ We reject the point $(1,-2)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-1,2).$" "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣y>0\\},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x+\\frac{2}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y & x+\\frac{2}{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{2}{-2x} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(-1)=2 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2).$ We reject the point $(1,-2)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-1,2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186434985 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186434986 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186434989 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186434990 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186434992 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186434997 :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=xy-{y}^{3},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=xy-{y}^{3},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186434997 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{3})=y, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{3})=x-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & x-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand ${f}^{′}$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}(-1) & (2)-3(-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{3})=y, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{3})=x-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & x-3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand ${f}^{′}$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) & (2)-3(-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186434998 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1),\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1),\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [:db/cas 17592186434998 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} & \\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{2}{3}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} & \\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{2}{3}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435062 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435063 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435064 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435068 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435071 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435080 :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $N$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[N\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The inverse of the matrix $N$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[N\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435080 :question/worked-solution "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $N$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[N\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${N}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $N$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[N\\,|\\,I] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${N}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435081 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}t-2 \\\\ 2-3t \\\\ 2t-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -3t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\,2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-2,2,-1)$  and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ 2-3t \\\\ 2t-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -3t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\,2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-2,2,-1)$  and is parallel to the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435082 :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=6u+4v+2,\\,y=5v-1\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 & a \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=6u+4v+2,\\,y=5v-1\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & a \\\\ 0 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435082 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6u+4v+2 \\\\ 5v-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6u+4v+2 \\\\ 5v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6u+4v \\\\ 5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+5+(-1)=8.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v+2 \\\\ 5v-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v+2 \\\\ 5v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+4v \\\\ 5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+5+(-1)=8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435147 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z=-1 \\\\ 3x-3y-z=5 \\\\ x-2y+3z=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-9-2)-(9+1)-2(-6+3) \\\\  & =-22-10+6 \\\\  & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & -10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -4 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}-26 \\\\ 26 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=1-1+1=1.$" "First, let's rewrite the system in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z=-1 \\\\ 3x-3y-z=5 \\\\ x-2y+3z=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we  write the system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-9-2)-(9+1)-2(-6+3) \\\\  & =-22-10+6 \\\\  & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & -10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -4 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 1 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-26 \\\\ 26 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y+z=1-1+1=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435149 :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z=3 \\\\ x-2y+z=1 \\\\ 2x-y+2z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z=3 \\\\ x-2y+z=1 \\\\ 2x-y+2z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435149 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=-1,y=1,z=4.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=-1+1+4=4.$" "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=-1,y=1,z=4.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=-1+1+4=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435169 :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=2u+5v,\\,y=u-3v\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ a & b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=2u+5v,\\,y=u-3v\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ a & b\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435169 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-3)+0=-2.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+5v \\\\ u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-3)+0=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435170 :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=4u+a,\\,y=6u+bv+c\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=4u+a,\\,y=6u+bv+c\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435170 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u \\\\ 6u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u \\\\ 6u+2v-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=4u+0,\\,y=6u+2v+(-1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+2+(-1)=1.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u \\\\ 6u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u \\\\ 6u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=4u+0,\\,y=6u+2v+(-1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+2+(-1)=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435171 :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=v-7u,\\,y=2v-u\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ -1 & b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=v-7u,\\,y=2v-u\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 \\\\ -1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435171 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}v-7u \\\\ 2v-u\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-7u \\\\ 2v-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7u+v \\\\ -u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-7+2+0=-5.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}v-7u \\\\ 2v-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-7u \\\\ 2v-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7u+v \\\\ -u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-7+2+0=-5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435356 :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435356 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435357 :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$, and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{4}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$, and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{4}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435357 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{4}}={{(\\frac{3}{4})}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{2} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{4}}={{(\\frac{3}{4})}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435358 :question/problem "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435358 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$" "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435376 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435378 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & y \\\\ y & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & y \\\\ y & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435379 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435380 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435382 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435384 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435385 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435386 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435388 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435389 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186435393 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & x+2y\\end{aligned}]$" "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & x+2y\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186435393 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & x+2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & x+2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435394 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(2,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2xy & 2y-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]$" "Find ${f}^{″}(2,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy & 2y-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186435394 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2xy & 2y-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2xy)=-2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=-2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-{x}^{2})=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2y & -2x \\\\ -2x & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(2,1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(2,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2xy & 2y-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2xy)=-2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=-2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-{x}^{2})=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2y & -2x \\\\ -2x & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(2,1).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(2,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435395 :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2} & 2y{x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1).$" "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2} & 2y{x}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186435395 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2} & 2y{x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2}-1)=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2})=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2}-1)=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-12.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2} & 2y{x}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2}-1)=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2})=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2}-1)=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435769 :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435770 :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435824 :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$ given that  $4a+3x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$ given that  $4a+3x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186435824 :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+3x & =b \\\\ 3x & =b-4a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(b-4a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -27 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2-(-9)=11.$" "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+3x & =b \\\\ 3x & =b-4a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(b-4a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -27 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2-(-9)=11.$"] [:db/cas 17592186435825 :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186435825 :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the second and the third equations we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we substitute into the second equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 4(-1)+3{x}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} & =12 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =-11 \\\\ 3(-1)-2(4) & =-11 \\\\ -11 & =-11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ which means\n\n$$\n-{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+4=3.\n$$" "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the second and the third equations we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we substitute into the second equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 4(-1)+3{x}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} & =12 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =-11 \\\\ 3(-1)-2(4) & =-11 \\\\ -11 & =-11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ which means\n\n$$\n-{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+4=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 175921