end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436072 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z \\\\ x-y-z\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y+z \\\\ x-y-z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436072 :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 3$ matrix $T$ such that\n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x+(-1)y+1z \\\\ 1x+(-1)y+(-1)z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z \\\\ x-y-z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We require a $2\\times 3$ matrix $T$ such that\n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+(-1)y+1z \\\\ 1x+(-1)y+(-1)z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y+z \\\\ x-y-z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436073 :question/problem "$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y+ax \\\\ bx+4y \\\\ cy+3x\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\,T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3y+ax \\\\ bx+4y \\\\ cy+3x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186436073 :question/worked-solution "Given the vector $\\,[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5x+3y \\\\ -2x+4y \\\\ 3x-y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3y+5x \\\\ -2x+4y \\\\ -y+3x\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y+5x \\\\ (-2)x+4y \\\\ (-1)y+3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+c=5+(-2)+(-1)=2.$" "Given the vector $\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5x+3y \\\\ -2x+4y \\\\ 3x-y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3y+5x \\\\ -2x+4y \\\\ -y+3x\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3y+5x \\\\ (-2)x+4y \\\\ (-1)y+3x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+c=5+(-2)+(-1)=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436074 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{4},$ where\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-4x-z+5y \\\\ 3x+7z-2y \\\\ -6x+y \\\\ 8y-3x\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{4},$ where\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-4x-z+5y \\\\ 3x+7z-2y \\\\ -6x+y \\\\ 8y-3x\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436074 :question/worked-solution "We require a $4\\times 3$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(-4)x+5y+(-1)z \\\\ 3x+(-2)y+7z \\\\ (-6)x+1y+0z \\\\ (-3)x+8y+0z\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4x-z+5y \\\\ 3x+7z-2y \\\\ -6x+y \\\\ 8y-3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We require a $4\\times 3$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-4)x+5y+(-1)z \\\\ 3x+(-2)y+7z \\\\ (-6)x+1y+0z \\\\ (-3)x+8y+0z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4x-z+5y \\\\ 3x+7z-2y \\\\ -6x+y \\\\ 8y-3x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436138 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436138 :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-8 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -18 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 10 & k-32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & k-32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-10{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-2.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-2\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$" "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-8 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -18 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 10 & k-32\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & k-32\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-10{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-2.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-2\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436236 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436237 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436238 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436239 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436240 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436241 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436242 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436243 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436244 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436245 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436248 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436248 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 4-6\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436249 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436249 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436448 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436449 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436451 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436453 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436454 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436457 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436458 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436459 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436461 :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}" "\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186436465 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186436465 :question/worked-solution "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2k+2 \\\\ 4k-4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2k+2=0 \\\\ 4k-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=1.$" "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2k+2 \\\\ 4k-4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2k+2=0 \\\\ 4k-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186436466 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186436466 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{2}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$" "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have one free variable, ${x}_{2}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436467 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -7 & 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186436467 :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -7 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have two free variables, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}-{x}_{3},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.\n$$" "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is ${x}_{1}$, and we have two free variables, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ in terms of ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nFrom the first equation we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}-{x}_{3},$ so the set of all the solutions of $Ax=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186436473 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436475 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436476 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 25\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 25\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436477 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436478 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436479 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436480 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436481 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186436483 :answer/value "[\