\\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440463 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186440463 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440464 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440464 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 7-12\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 7-12\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440465 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440465 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 6-(-4)\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 6-(-4)\\cdot (-5)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440466 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440466 :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440467 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186440467 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -13 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -13 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -13 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -13 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440468 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186440468 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 13 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 13 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 13 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440469 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440469 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440470 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186440470 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-6\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186440655 :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{11}{a}_{21}+{a}_{12}{a}_{22}>0.$ However, for the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have ${a}_{11}{a}_{21}+{a}_{12}{a}_{22}=1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1=0≯0.$ So, ${I}_{2}\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, let $\\alpha =0\\in R$ and $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in T.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\alpha \\cdot A=0\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere the sum of the products of columns' entries is $0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$ Hence, $0\\cdot A\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "Let's verify the statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{11}{a}_{21}+{a}_{12}{a}_{22}>0.$ However, for the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have ${a}_{11}{a}_{21}+{a}_{12}{a}_{22}=1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1=0≯0.$ So, ${I}_{2}\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, let $\\alpha =0\\in R$ and $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in T.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\alpha \\cdot A=0\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere the sum of the products of columns' entries is $0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$ Hence, $0\\cdot A\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186440906 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186440912 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186440913 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186440921 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186440925 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186440932 :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$, then ${A}^{4}=$" "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${A}^{4}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186440932 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${A}^{2}=A,$ so $A$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${A}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & ={A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, we compute ${A}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${A}^{2}=A,$ so $A$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${A}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & ={A}^{2} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186441352 :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186441352 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441353 :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186441353 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186441354 :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186441354 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186441355 :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $B=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186441355 :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186441356 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186441356 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=5,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=5.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=5,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441581 :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441583 :question/problem "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$" "Let $c=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $d=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441587 :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $b$ onto $a.$" "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $b$ onto $a.$"] [:db/cas 17592186441588 :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186442108 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442109 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442110 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442111 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442112 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442114 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-10)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]+10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-10)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-10)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-10)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186442218 :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ {e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at  $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ 1 & ∗\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ {e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 1 \\\\ 1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?"] [:db/cas 17592186442218 :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}})=2{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}})={e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}})={e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}})=-2{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} & {e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ {e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}} & -2{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+2+2=5.\n$$" "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}})=2{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}})={e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}})={e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}})=-2{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} & {e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ {e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}} & -2{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+2+2=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186442271 :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $B$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =-5$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $B$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =-5$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$"] [:db/cas 17592186442271 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd column, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )((3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda +3)({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0$ for the polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7$, we have that the only real eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =-3$ (both with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -3-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd column, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )\\cdot (-3-\\lambda )((3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda +3)({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0$ for the polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +7$, we have that the only real eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =-3$ (both with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186442272 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186442272 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+15\\lambda )+(-5-\\lambda )+(5+\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda -10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $10:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 5,\\pm 10\n$$\n\nLet's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}+2(1)^{2}-13(1)+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the equation. So, we can factor $(\\lambda -1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-13$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-3-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+15\\lambda )+(-5-\\lambda )+(5+\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda -10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $10:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 5,\\pm 10\n$$\n\nLet's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}+2(1)^{2}-13(1)+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the equation. So, we can factor $(\\lambda -1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-13$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $-10$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186442296 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & a & b \\\\ 5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ c \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & a & b \\\\ 5 & -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ c \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186442296 :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 20 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=20+(-10)+(-6)=4.\n$$" "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 20 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=20+(-10)+(-6)=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186442485 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442489 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442491 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442496 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442497 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442498 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442499 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442501 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2} & -{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & {e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{-2} & -{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & {e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442502 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442504 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442507 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2} & -{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{2} & 3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2} & -{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{2} & 3{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442510 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442513 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4} & {e}^{4} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4} & {e}^{4} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442515 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442521 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442524 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442525 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442529 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442533 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 & -{e}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡1 \\\\ {e}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡1 & {e}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 & -{e}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡1 \\\\ {e}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡1 & {e}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442536 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186442556 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"] [:db/cas 17592186442556 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}=\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."] [:db/cas 17592186442557 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"] [:db/cas 17592186442557 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental matrix could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental matrix could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."] [:db/cas 17592186442558 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186442558 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+3\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((3\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(3\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(3\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+3\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186442559 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186442559 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}\\cdot t+\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}\\cdot t+\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}\\cdot t+\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{t} \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{t}}{2t}\\cdot t+\\frac{3{e}^{t}-{e}^{3t}}{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186442560 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186442560 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-t)={e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(-t)={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-t{e}^{-t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}(1+t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-{e}^{-t}t+{e}^{-t}(1+t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{-t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-t)={e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(-t)={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-t{e}^{-t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}(1+t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-{e}^{-t}t+{e}^{-t}(1+t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{-t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186442562 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?"] [:db/cas 17592186442562 :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."] [:db/cas 17592186442766 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442767 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442768 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442771 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442773 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442774 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442775 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442777 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442779 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442781 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442782 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442785 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442786 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442787 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442788 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442789 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442790 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442791 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442793 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442794 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186442799 :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(D).$" "Consider the matrix \n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(D).$"] [:db/cas 17592186442799 :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(D),$ we reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${d}_{1}$ and ${d}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(D),$ we reduce the matrix $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${d}_{1}$ and ${d}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186442800 :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 4 & -10 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(T).$" "Consider the matrix \n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 4 & -10 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186442800 :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(T),$ we reduce the matrix $T$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 4 & -10 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 4 & -10 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${t}_{1},$ ${t}_{2}$ and ${t}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is $\\{{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(T),$ we reduce the matrix $T$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 4 & -10 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 4 & -10 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${t}_{1},$ ${t}_{2}$ and ${t}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is $\\{{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186442801 :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 2 \\\\ -9 & 17 & -10\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(T).$" "Consider the matrix \n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -7 & 2 \\\\ -9 & 17 & -10\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(T).$"]]
